INT. HALL OF MIRRORS - OFF-SEASON FAIRGROUND - NIGHT

A canvas-walled maze of warped glass. Half the bulbs are dead. Dust coats every frame. The mirrors turn the dark into a hundred darks.

CORINNE DUBOIS-ADEYEMI, thirties, a paranormal investigator in a leather jacket, walks slowly down a corridor with a handheld lamp, her reflection stretching tall, then squat, then tall again.

ARTHUR "ARTIE" PLUMLEY, seventies, the fairground's last attendant, shuffles behind her in a patched coat, a ring of keys swinging at his hip.

CORINNE
Artie, you said the last visitor left in 1987.

ARTIE
So I did.

CORINNE
Then who is the person in the third mirror?

ARTIE
Miss Dubois-Adeyemi, I wouldn't look.

She looks. Her reflection waves, but she hasn't moved.

CORINNE
Artie.

ARTIE
Yes, miss.

CORINNE
Is that me?

ARTIE
It was. For a while.

She steps back. Her reflection steps forward. The glass ripples like water.

CORINNE
Artie, open the door.

ARTIE
There's no door, miss. There's only the way you came.

CORINNE
Then take me back.

ARTIE
I'd love to. But I've been looking for the way out since the summer of '87.

She turns. Behind her, in every mirror, Artie stands younger, smiling, and a hundred reflections of her are walking away.

CORINNE
(a whisper)
Artie, what did you do?

ARTIE
I stayed too long, miss. Same as you.

The lamp gutters out.
